00:07:20.110 --> 00:07:23.530
Uniform gate and candidate packages are the default recurrent cell,

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:25.510
and that is where we pop the hood.

00:07:26.110 --> 00:07:29.050
A conventional cell pays for full recurrent gate matrices

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:31.750
and a candidate transform as one bundle.

00:07:32.370 --> 00:07:36.370
Most of the parameter budget can sit in a single dense candidate matrix instead.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:39.270
Gates then shrink to cheap diagonal interactions.

00:07:39.950 --> 00:07:42.590
Candidate generation keeps an H by H matrix.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:45.230
The gate side becomes all of H terms.

00:07:45.710 --> 00:07:49.830
A lightweight second order path also reads hidden state at T minus 2.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:55.050
On text 8, loss stays within a few hundreds of a bit per character of full gate baselines.

00:07:55.690 --> 00:07:59.210
Bit per character is the usual language model unit on that benchmark.

00:07:59.730 --> 00:08:01.970
So the gap against full gate cells is small.

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:06.770
Once hidden size grows, the dense candidate matrix is no longer the bottleneck.

00:08:06.770 --> 00:08:11.850
Streaming and on-device sequence models should measure whether the workload is candidate-limited

00:08:11.850 --> 00:08:15.210
before defaulting to a full LSTM or GRU.

00:08:15.670 --> 00:08:20.590
When it is, reallocating capacity this way yields lower loss and cheaper matrix vector

00:08:20.590 --> 00:08:21.050
work.

